Review record for two changes: the missouri install step, and the race
in the gate test helper.

The first replaces one install command in the release workflow with the
action that already installs missouri on every pull request. The second
accepts a broken pipe in the test helper that drives the gate's
configuration refusals. No product code changes. The driver verified
both directly. Each line states what was checked.

signoff[review-tests] PASS 747c894 `cargo test --test merge_gate_guard` runs green here after the change, and the case that failed in the release check is the one the change covers, "the ref line writes: Broken pipe" with the other eleven green; the assertion narrows rather than widens, because only `ErrorKind::BrokenPipe` passes and every other error still fails the test; no test reads a workflow file, so the workflow half was exercised by a dispatch of the release workflow on this branch, which runs every build job and skips every publish job
signoff[review-code] PASS 747c894 the missouri action was read at cjohnhanson/missouri action.yml, and it runs `cargo install --git ... --rev <sha> --locked --root ~/.missouri-tools missouri`, which names the package cargo asked for; the verify job's step list was printed from the edited file with a YAML reader and the action sits before the suite run; the helper change is scoped to the one call site that can exit before it reads stdin, and the two helpers that run in a valid repository still treat any write error as a failure
signoff[review-docs] PASS 747c894 each change carries a comment stating the cause: a fixture in missouri is itself a package with a binary, and the configuration checks run before the gate reads stdin; no document states the release workflow's install command or the helper's error handling
signoff[review-deps] PASS 747c894 no manifest and no lockfile changes in either commit; the action is the one the pull request workflow already runs, and it pins missouri to a resolved sha rather than a floating branch, which is narrower than the command it replaces
signoff[review-usability] PASS 747c894 no binary, help text or error message changes; the reader affected is whoever cuts the next tag, and what they met was a red verify job, once on a cargo error naming both packages and once on a race that reported a broken pipe rather than the refusal under test
